{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "וכהן", :translit "u-chohen", :gloss "and the priest"} {:he "וכהן כי יקנה", :translit "u-chohen ki yikneh", :gloss "and the priest when he acquires"} {:he "וכהן", :translit "ve-chohen", :gloss "and a priest"} {:he "ובלחמו", :translit "u-vi-lachmo", :gloss "and on his bread"} {:he "ובלחמו", :translit "ve-lachmo", :gloss "its bread"} {:he "ונודד", :translit "ve-nad", :gloss "and you shall wander"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["וכהן כי יקנה" "מלך וכהן"]} {:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["בלחמו" "בלחמו"]} {:kind :other, :note "Word-linkage (gezeirah shavah): shared rare term בלחמו links to Genesis 4:12 נודד"}], :frame {:text "The Gemara at Yoma 19a records that the Temple courtyard cried out four cries. One was Psalm 24:7, \"Lift up your heads, O gates,\" upon which Ishmael ben Phiabi entered and served as High Priest; he was righteous, many ate at his table, and no notar (leftover sacrificial meat) was found in his days — taken as a sign of abundant offerings properly consumed. The Ba'al HaTurim reads the phrase מלך וכהן in Ecclesiastes 5:5 as alluding to King Yannai (Alexander Jannaeus), a Hasmonean ruler condemned in rabbinic literature for slaughtering sages (Sukkah 56b mentions his wickedness). Genesis 4:12 records the curse נודד תהיה בארץ, \"a wanderer shall you be on the earth.\"", :sources ["Yoma 19a" "Psalms 24:7" "Sukkah 56b" "Genesis 4:12" "Job 14:7" "Ecclesiastes 5:5" "Leviticus 22:11"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["וכהן כי יקנה" "מלך וכהן"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "וכהן כי יקנה", :refs ["Leviticus 22:11"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "מלך וכהן", :refs ["Lamentations 2:6"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["בלחמו" "בלחמו"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "בלחמו", :refs ["Leviticus 22:11" "Isaiah 21:14" "Jeremiah 11:19"], :n 3, :lens :exact} {:phrase "בלחמו", :refs ["Leviticus 22:11" "Isaiah 21:14" "Jerem